(meta (title "Made when read, kept until changed")
      (date 2026-09-24))

Nothing here was built beforehand. The first time a page is asked for,
glasnost reads its @code{.sz}, makes the HTML, and keeps it. After that it
serves what it kept, until something the page was made from changes.

@toc

@section{What a page is made from}

Beside each kept page is a list of the files it was made from: its own
@code{.sz}, and everything that includes, like the last lines of this note. A
folder's page is made from the folder itself, its @code{index.sz}, and
everything its menu says something about. When any of them has been written
since the page was made, or is gone, the page is made again.

@section{What made it}

The pages are kept in a folder named by a fingerprint of the code that made
them: glasnost, samizdat, and what they use. A glasnost built from other code
keeps its pages somewhere else, and so never serves a page that older code
made. When it starts, it removes the caches older code left behind.

@section{When it cannot}

A page that cannot be made --- a command that does not exist, an include that
is not hidden --- is not kept. It is answered with a page that says what went
wrong and where, as samizdat said it:

@codeblock|{
notes/broken.sz:3:11: @nosuchcommand: there is no such command
}|

Because of @ref["what-a-page-is-made-from"]{the list of sources}, fixing the
file fixes the page: the next time it is read, it is made again.

@include["../_parts/signature.sz"]
